Answer: The super keyword refers to the immediate parent class. It is mainly used to access parent class variables and methods when they are hidden or overridden in the child class.

```java
class Demo1
{
int a = 10;
```

void display()

```java
{
System.out.println("This display is from Demo1");
}
}
class Demo2 extends Demo1
{
int a = 50;
```

void display()

```java
{
System.out.println("Child a = " + a);
System.out.println("Parent a = " + super.a);
super.display();
}
}
```

Here, a refers to the child class variable, while super.a refers to the parent class variable.

Answer: The super keyword can be used to call the immediate parent class version of a method.

```java
class Demo1
{
```

