}
}
```

super.display() executes the method defined in the parent class.

Answer: A final class is a class that cannot be inherited by another class. It is used when further inheritance from a particular class should not be allowed.

```java
final class SerpentEagle
{
```

void fly()

```java
{
System.out.println("Eagle is flying very high.");
}
}
class Lion extends SerpentEagle
{
// Compilation error
}
```

Lion cannot extend SerpentEagle because SerpentEagle is declared as final.

Answer: A final method can be inherited by a child class, but it cannot be overridden.

```java
class Eagle
{
final void fly()
{
System.out.println("Eagle is flying very high.");
}
}
class SerpentEagle extends Eagle
{
// fly() cannot be overridden here
}
```

If SerpentEagle tries to provide another implementation of fly(), the compiler reports an error.

