Answer: A final variable can be assigned only once. After initialization, its value cannot be changed.

```java
final int age = 10;
// age = 20;  // Compilation error
```

Answer: Yes. A final local variable can be declared first and initialized later, as long as it is assigned before it is used.

```java
final int age;
age = 20;
System.out.println(age);
```

After the first assignment, it cannot be assigned another value.

Answer: A final instance variable is an instance variable whose value cannot be changed after initialization. It must be initialized appropriately before it is used.

```java
class Student
{
final int age = 20;
```

void display()

```java
{
System.out.println(age);
}
}
```

